From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 00:20:56 +0200
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: split the GRX550 facts out of the FRITZ!Box
 7590

The FRITZ!Box 7590 board file carries a set of statements that are not
about the board at all. The fifth GPHY, and the WAN port it serves on
the second GSWIP instance, exist on the GRX550 die and not on the GRX350
one. Both USB controllers with the single VBUS line that powers them,
and the SSO shift register the front panel hangs off, are how AVM wires
every GRX550 box rather than how it wires this one. Each of them was
written into this file because this was the only GRX550 board here.

It is not any more. Put them in seale_avm_grx550.dtsi, between the
board-family layer and the board, and have the FRITZ!Box 7590 include that
instead. AVM's own tree splits at exactly this line -- its
grx550_common.dtsi holds the same set -- which is the best evidence
available that the boundary is a real one and not a convenience.

seale_avm.dtsi's own note anticipated the move and named the trigger: a
second GRX550 board. There is deliberately no seale_avm_grx350.dtsi,
because the FRITZ!Box 7560 remains the only board on that die and such a
file would be a second name for its board file.

The comment on the SSO controller anticipated it too. It explained that
the block and its pin group sat in a board file so a malformed pin group
could not reach the FRITZ!Box 7560's pinctrl probe, which fails whole
rather than per group, and said to promote both the day a second board
wired the function. A file the FRITZ!Box 7560 does not include keeps
that guarantee just as well.

What stays behind is board data: identity, memory, crash persistence, the
flash map, which of the eight SSO pins are populated and what each lamp is
called, the three buttons, and the DSL companion behind RC2.

Pure refactor. seale_avm_fritz7590.dtb is semantically unchanged: 90
nodes, 520 properties and 26 phandles compare equal property by property,
with phandle values canonicalised to the node paths they point at. Only
the order dtc emits nodes in moves, so the blob is the same 14599 bytes
and different bytes. seale_avm_fritz7560.dtb and easy350_anywan.dtb are
byte-identical.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

